CONTEXT FROM TEXTBOOK:
This figure is a schematic drawing of a polarimeter, laid out as a single optical path running left to right in perspective. At the far left a lit incandescent bulb sits in a socket inside a yellow glow, labeled "Light source", and short red double-headed arrows pointing in many directions emerge from it beneath the label "Unpolarized light". The beam is drawn as a flat pale yellow ribbon that passes through a circular disc with fine vertical striping, labeled "Polarizer", after which the red arrows all lie in a single vertical plane under the label "Polarized light". The ribbon then runs through a horizontal cylinder with a small filling stub on top, drawn with rounded ends and labeled "Sample tube containing organic molecules"; the red arrows inside progressively tip away from vertical as they move along the tube. Beyond the tube the beam meets a second striped disc labeled "Analyzer" and continues to the right, where a heavy red double-headed arrow lies at a slant. A vertical dashed reference line and a curved arrow mark the angle between the two, labeled with the Greek letter alpha, and a drawn eye at the right edge is labeled "Observer". Tracking one ribbon of light through every component makes the tilt of the arrows the quantity to follow. The arrangement shows how the rotation an optically active sample imposes on the plane of polarization is measured.

PROJECTION TYPE: perspective
Use the scaffold's true perspective projection after building all geometry. Use this only when the source has real vanishing-point convergence or distance-based size shrinkage; then depth should visibly affect apparent size.

ELEMENTS TO RECREATE IN 3D:
  - Incandescent bulb with glass envelope, filament, and screw base seated in a small round socket/stand at far left
  - Soft yellow radial glow halo around the bulb
  - Cluster of short red double-headed arrows radiating in many orientations just right of the bulb (unpolarized light)
  - Flat pale-yellow translucent ribbon representing the light beam, running left to right along the optical axis through every component
  - Polarizer: upright circular disc with thin dark vertical stripes and a grey rim, centered on the beam
  - Vertical red double-headed arrows in the beam between polarizer and sample tube (plane-polarized light)
  - Sample tube: horizontal glass/grey cylinder with rounded (domed) ends, semi-transparent, containing a faint pale-green liquid
  - Small cylindrical filling stub on top of the sample tube, near its left-of-center top surface
  - Sequence of red double-headed arrows inside the tube whose tilt increases progressively from vertical at the entrance to the final rotated angle at the exit
  - Analyzer: second upright striped circular disc with grey rim, downstream of the tube
  - Outgoing yellow ribbon continuing right of the analyzer
  - Heavy slanted red double-headed arrow at the exit representing the rotated plane of polarization
  - Vertical dashed reference line at the exit marking 0° (original polarization plane)
  - Curved arrow spanning from the dashed line to the tilted red arrow with Greek letter α label
  - Stylized eye drawn at far right (observer)
  - Text labels: 'Unpolarized light', 'Light source', 'Polarizer', 'Polarized light', 'Sample tube containing organic molecules', 'Analyzer', 'Observer', 'α'

GEOMETRY NOTES (world-space scale, color palette, and any formula/value needed for correctness — build to these so sizes, zoom, and colors match the source; do not guess dimensions the notes already specify):
Optical axis along +X, world units: total path ~16 units from bulb at x=-8 to observer's eye at x=+8. Bulb: sphere r=0.55 at x=-7.4, y=0.5, warm yellow #FFE45C emissive, with grey screw base and a pale disc socket (r=0.7, thickness 0.15) at y=-0.4; glow halo = soft yellow sprite r≈1.8 at 25% opacity. Unpolarized arrow cluster: 8 red (#E8232A) double-headed arrows length 0.5, centered at x=-6.2, randomly oriented in the YZ plane plus a few oblique. Beam ribbon: flat plane 0.9 wide in Z, running x=-6.5 to +6.0, pale yellow #F2D98C at ~60% opacity, lying horizontally (in the XZ plane) so it reads as a flat sheet seen from slightly above. Polarizer disc: circle r=1.0, thickness 0.1, centered x=-4.6, standing upright in the YZ plane (normal along X), grey rim #9A9A9A, face filled with ~24 thin dark vertical (Y-direction) stripes on olive-green #7A8A3C. Polarized arrows: 3 vertical red double-headed arrows length 1.4 at x=-4.0, -3.3, -2.6. Sample tube: cylinder radius 0.85, length 4.4, axis along X, centered at x=-0.6, semi-transparent pale grey-green #CFD9CC at 45% opacity, with hemispherical/domed caps at both ends and a small stub cylinder r=0.18, height 0.4 on top at x=-1.2. Inside the tube place 6 red double-headed arrows length 1.3 at x = -2.6, -1.9, -1.2, -0.5, 0.2, 0.9, each rotated about the X axis by fraction i/5 of the sample rotation angle so the twist accumulates left to right. Analyzer disc: identical to polarizer, r=1.0, centered x=2.6, its stripe pattern rotating about X with analyzerAngle. Exit region: heavy red double-headed arrow length 2.0, thickness 0.08, centered x=4.4, tilted in the YZ plane by the sample rotation angle. Vertical grey dashed line from y=-1.3 to y=+1.3 at x=4.4. Curved α arc radius 1.1 in the YZ plane from the dashed vertical to the red arrow, with italic 'α' label just outside the arc. Observer eye: stylized almond outline with dark pupil at x=7.4, y=0, plus two short horizontal grey sight lines running left from it. Palette: red arrows #E8232A, beam #F2D98C, glow #FFF2A0, disc rims and tube hardware #A8A8A8, tube green tint #BFD2BC, text charcoal #222222. Sign convention: positive sample rotation tilts the arrow clockwise as seen by the observer looking back toward the source (dextrorotatory).

DISCRETE CONTROLS (implement every one of these in #ui, each must work independently):
[
  {
    "id": "sampleRotation",
    "type": "slider",
    "label": "Sample optical rotation (°)",
    "range": [
      -60,
      60,
      1
    ],
    "default": 30,
    "teaches": "An optically active sample twists the plane of plane-polarized light; the arrows inside the tube tip progressively and the exit plane angle equals the sample's rotation, with sign giving dextro- vs levorotatory."
  },
  {
    "id": "analyzerAngle",
    "type": "slider",
    "label": "Analyzer rotation (°)",
    "range": [
      -90,
      90,
      1
    ],
    "default": 0,
    "teaches": "The observer measures α by rotating the analyzer until light is transmitted maximally; the reading only matches the true rotation when the analyzer's transmission axis lines up with the exit plane."
  },
  {
    "id": "beamPulse",
    "type": "animation",
    "label": "Send a pulse of light down the path",
    "loop": true,
    "teaches": "Each component acts in turn: the source emits arrows in all orientations, the polarizer keeps only the vertical plane, and the sample tube gradually rotates that plane before it reaches the analyzer."
  },
  {
    "id": "orbitView",
    "type": "orbit",
    "teaches": "The beam is a plane of oscillation in 3D, not a flat line on the page — rotating the scene shows the polarization plane standing perpendicular to the propagation direction."
  }
]

DEMO STEPS (tween through these using goToStep(); each step drives the controls above):
[
  {
    "title": "Unpolarized light leaves the source",
    "narration": "The bulb emits light whose electric field oscillates in every orientation, shown as red double-headed arrows pointing in many directions.",
    "state": {
      "sampleRotation": 0,
      "analyzerAngle": 0
    }
  },
  {
    "title": "Polarizer selects one plane",
    "narration": "After the striped polarizer only the vertical plane survives; the arrows between polarizer and tube are all vertical.",
    "state": {
      "sampleRotation": 0,
      "analyzerAngle": 0
    }
  },
  {
    "title": "An inactive sample changes nothing",
    "narration": "With optical rotation set to zero, the arrows stay vertical through the whole tube and α = 0°; the analyzer at 0° passes the beam fully.",
    "state": {
      "sampleRotation": 0,
      "analyzerAngle": 0
    }
  },
  {
    "title": "A chiral sample rotates the plane",
    "narration": "Set a positive rotation: the arrows tip further and further as they travel through the tube, leaving at +30° from vertical.",
    "state": {
      "sampleRotation": 30,
      "analyzerAngle": 0
    }
  },
  {
    "title": "The observer finds α",
    "narration": "Rotating the analyzer to +30° restores maximum brightness — the angle read off the dashed reference line is α, the rotation imposed by the sample.",
    "state": {
      "sampleRotation": 30,
      "analyzerAngle": 30
    }
  },
  {
    "title": "Opposite enantiomer, opposite sign",
    "narration": "A rotation of −30° tips the arrows the other way; the analyzer must now be turned to −30°, showing that α carries a sign.",
    "state": {
      "sampleRotation": -30,
      "analyzerAngle": -30
    }
  }
]

CAMERA VIEW (framing only). Call setCameraView({ projection, ...these }) AFTER all geometry is added. Keep the projection MECHANISM from PROJECTION TYPE above (orthographic / sheared-oblique / perspective). For perspective these params set the pose (azimuth/elevation/roll); for the two parallel projections pose is a standard default (see PROJECTION TYPE above), and these params set only roll and framing (heightFraction):
{
  "azimuthDeg": 20,
  "elevationDeg": 18,
  "rollDeg": 0,
  "heightFraction": 0.6
}

NOTES: Render discs as CircleGeometry with a CanvasTexture of vertical stripes; rotate the texture (or the mesh about the X axis) for the analyzer so the striping visibly turns with the slider. Use MeshPhysicalMaterial with transmission/opacity for the sample tube and a DoubleSide transparent MeshBasicMaterial for the beam ribbon so it stays flat and non-shaded. Build each double-headed arrow as a thin cylinder plus two cone tips grouped, then set group.rotation.x to the local polarization angle — makes both sliders and the pulse animation cheap. The glow is an additive-blended sprite, not real lighting. Labels via CSS2DRenderer anchored to their components with small leader lines matching the source (curved leader from 'Sample tube...' up to the tube). The pulse animation drives a normalized t from 0→1 that moves a brighter ribbon segment plus a traveling arrow whose orientation is: random per frame for x<polarizer, vertical between polarizer and tube entrance, lerped to sampleRotation across the tube, fixed after. Analyzer transmission brightness = cos^2(analyzerAngle - sampleRotation) applied to the exit ribbon opacity so misalignment visibly dims the beam. Keep OrbitControls damped with limited polar range so the schematic never flips upside down.

Follow the interaction plan above.

The second image above is a screenshot of the PREVIOUS attempt's rendered output (not the source figure) — use it to see exactly what broke.

Here is the generated code to evaluate:

<!-- @FIGURE_UI_BEGIN -->
<div style="font-weight:bold;font-size:12px">Polarimeter</div>
<label>Sample optical rotation &alpha; (&deg;): <b id="valSample">30</b>
  <input type="range" id="sampleRotation" min="-60" max="60" step="1" value="30">
</label>
<label>Analyzer rotation (&deg;): <b id="valAnalyzer">0</b>
  <input type="range" id="analyzerAngle" min="-90" max="90" step="1" value="0">
</label>
<div style="font-size:11px;line-height:1.45">
  Exit plane tilt&nbsp;=&nbsp;<b id="valAlpha">+30&deg;</b><br>
  Transmitted intensity&nbsp;=&nbsp;cos<sup>2</sup>(analyzer &minus; &alpha;)&nbsp;=&nbsp;<b id="valTrans">75%</b>
</div>
<button id="btnPulse">&#9654; Send a pulse of light</button>
<label style="flex-direction:row;align-items:center;gap:5px">
  <input type="checkbox" id="chkPlane" checked> Show polarization plane
</label>
<div style="display:flex;gap:4px;margin-top:2px">
  <button id="btnFigView">Figure view</button>
  <button id="btnObsView">Observer's view</button>
</div>
<div style="display:flex;gap:4px;margin-top:4px">
  <button id="btnPrev">&#9664; Prev</button>
  <button id="btnNext">Next &#9654;</button>
</div>
<div id="narration" style="font-size:11px;line-height:1.45;margin-top:2px"></div>
<div style="font-size:10px;color:#666;line-height:1.4;margin-top:4px">
  Drag to orbit: the red double-headed arrows stand in the plane perpendicular to the beam, so turning the
  scene shows the polarization plane in 3D. "Observer's view" looks back along the beam, where the angle
  &alpha; appears at its true size. Hover a component for a note.
</div>
<!-- @FIGURE_UI_END -->
// @FIGURE_CODE_BEGIN
/* ── Element spec (build exactly this) ───────────────────────────────────────
 bulb envelope        → SphereGeometry(0.55)              MeshBasic  #FFE45C
 filament             → Line (zig-zag)                    #B07A10
 screw base           → CylinderGeometry(.28,.24,.42)     MeshStandard #A8A8A8
 socket disc          → CylinderGeometry(.70,.78,.16)     MeshStandard #EAEAEA
 glow halo            → Sprite (radial gradient canvas)   r≈1.6
 unpolarized star     → 8 × makeArrow(0.9, r=.055) #E8232A in YZ plane at x=-6.2
 beam ribbon (in)     → PlaneGeometry(9.1,0.9) in XZ      MeshBasic #F2D98C 62%
 beam ribbon (out)    → PlaneGeometry(3.4,0.9) in XZ      opacity = cos²Δ
 polarizer            → CircleGeometry(1.0)+stripe CanvasTexture + rim Cylinder #A8A8A8
 polarized arrows     → 3 × makeArrow(1.4) vertical at x=-4.0,-3.3,-2.6
 sample tube          → open CylinderGeometry(.85,.85,4.4) + 2 domes MeshPhysical 40%
 tube liquid          → CylinderGeometry(.78,.78,4.2)     #BFD2BC 28%
 fill stub            → CylinderGeometry(.18,.18,.4)      #A8A8A8 on top
 tube arrows          → 6 × makeArrow(1.35,r=.055), rotation.x = -(i/5)·α
 twist ribbon         → BufferGeometry strip, twists 0→α  #E8232A 16%
 analyzer             → same as polarizer, group.rotation.x = -analyzerAngle
 exit arrow           → makeArrow(2.1, r=.10), rotation.x = -α
 exit plane           → PlaneGeometry(3.0,1.9) in XY, rotation.x = -α, faint red
 dashed reference     → LineDashedMaterial vertical at x=4.4
 α arc                → TubeGeometry(arc r=1.28) + ConeGeometry head + wedge fan (YZ)
 observer eye         → almond LineLoop (billboarded) + CircleGeometry pupil + sight lines
 leader lines         → Line / QuadraticBezierCurve3, grey
────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── */

const RED = 0xE8232A, BEAM = 0xF2D98C, GREY = 0xA8A8A8, INK = '#222222';

scene.add(new THREE.AmbientLight(0xffffff, 0.92));
const dl = new THREE.DirectionalLight(0xffffff, 0.55); dl.position.set(2, 6, 8); scene.add(dl);
const dl2 = new THREE.DirectionalLight(0xffffff, 0.3); dl2.position.set(-4, 2, -6); scene.add(dl2);

/* ── helpers ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── */
function makeArrow(len, color, rad) {
  rad = rad || 0.04;
  const g = new THREE.Group();
  const mat = new THREE.MeshBasicMaterial({ color: color });
  const hl = Math.min(rad * 6.0, len * 0.3);
  const shaft = new THREE.Mesh(new THREE.CylinderGeometry(rad, rad, Math.max(0.01, len - 2 * hl), 12), mat);
  g.add(shaft);
  const cg = new THREE.ConeGeometry(rad * 3.1, hl, 16);
  const top = new THREE.Mesh(cg, mat); top.position.y = len / 2 - hl / 2; g.add(top);
  const bot = new THREE.Mesh(cg, mat); bot.position.y = -(len / 2 - hl / 2); bot.rotation.x = Math.PI; g.add(bot);
  return g;
}

function stripeTexture() {
  const cv = document.createElement('canvas'); cv.width = 256; cv.height = 256;
  const x = cv.getContext('2d');
  x.fillStyle = '#93A24C'; x.fillRect(0, 0, 256, 256);
  x.strokeStyle = '#2B3512'; x.lineWidth = 4;
  for (let i = 0; i < 26; i++) {
    const px = (i + 0.5) * 256 / 26;
    x.beginPath(); x.moveTo(px, 0); x.lineTo(px, 256); x.stroke();
  }
  const t = new THREE.CanvasTexture(cv);
  t.anisotropy = 4;
  return t;
}

// Disc: outer group rotates about X (beam axis); inner mesh faces +X.
function makeDisc(xPos) {
  const outer = new THREE.Group(); outer.position.x = xPos;
  const inner = new THREE.Group(); inner.rotation.y = Math.PI / 2; outer.add(inner);
  const faceMat = new THREE.MeshBasicMaterial({ map: stripeTexture(), side: THREE.DoubleSide });
  const face = new THREE.Mesh(new THREE.CircleGeometry(1.0, 64), faceMat);
  inner.add(face);
  const rimMat = new THREE.MeshStandardMaterial({ color: 0xBEBEBE, roughness: 0.4, metalness: 0.25, side: THREE.DoubleSide });
  const rim = new THREE.Mesh(new THREE.CylinderGeometry(1.06, 1.06, 0.14, 64, 1, true), rimMat);
  rim.rotation.z = Math.PI / 2; outer.add(rim);
  const rimFace = new THREE.Mesh(new THREE.RingGeometry(0.99, 1.08, 64), rimMat);
  rimFace.rotation.y = Math.PI / 2; rimFace.position.x = 0.071; outer.add(rimFace);
  const rimFace2 = rimFace.clone(); rimFace2.position.x = -0.071; outer.add(rimFace2);
  scene.add(outer);
  return outer;
}

function greyLine(pts, color, dashed) {
  const g = new THREE.BufferGeometry().setFromPoints(pts);
  const m = dashed
    ? new THREE.LineDashedMaterial({ color: color, dashSize: 0.17, gapSize: 0.13 })
    : new THREE.LineBasicMaterial({ color: color });
  const l = new THREE.Line(g, m);
  if (dashed) l.computeLineDistances();
  scene.add(l);
  return l;
}

/* ── light source ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── */
const sourceGroup = new THREE.Group(); scene.add(sourceGroup);

const bulb = new THREE.Mesh(
  new THREE.SphereGeometry(0.55, 32, 24),
  new THREE.MeshBasicMaterial({ color: 0xFFE45C })
);
bulb.position.set(-7.4, 0.5, 0); sourceGroup.add(bulb);

const filPts = [];
for (let i = 0; i <= 8; i++) filPts.push(new THREE.Vector3(-7.4 + (i % 2 ? 0.11 : -0.11), 0.22 + i * 0.05, 0));
sourceGroup.add(new THREE.Line(new THREE.BufferGeometry().setFromPoints(filPts),
  new THREE.LineBasicMaterial({ color: 0xB07A10 })));

const baseMat = new THREE.MeshStandardMaterial({ color: GREY, roughness: 0.4, metalness: 0.45 });
const screw = new THREE.Mesh(new THREE.CylinderGeometry(0.28, 0.24, 0.42, 20), baseMat);
screw.position.set(-7.4, -0.12, 0); sourceGroup.add(screw);
const socket = new THREE.Mesh(new THREE.CylinderGeometry(0.7, 0.78, 0.16, 32),
  new THREE.MeshStandardMaterial({ color: 0xEAEAEA, roughness: 0.5 }));
socket.position.set(-7.4, -0.42, 0); sourceGroup.add(socket);

// glow sprite — NORMAL blending so it is visible on the white background
(function () {
  const cv = document.createElement('canvas'); cv.width = 160; cv.height = 160;
  const ctx = cv.getContext('2d');
  const g = ctx.createRadialGradient(80, 80, 6, 80, 80, 80);
  g.addColorStop(0, 'rgba(255,242,140,0.85)');
  g.addColorStop(0.35, 'rgba(255,240,130,0.45)');
  g.addColorStop(0.7, 'rgba(255,240,150,0.14)');
  g.addColorStop(1, 'rgba(255,245,180,0)');
  ctx.fillStyle = g; ctx.fillRect(0, 0, 160, 160);
  const sp = new THREE.Sprite(new THREE.SpriteMaterial({
    map: new THREE.CanvasTexture(cv), transparent: true, opacity: 0.95, depthWrite: false
  }));
  sp.scale.set(3.2, 3.2, 1); sp.position.set(-7.4, 0.45, -0.05); sourceGroup.add(sp);
})();

registerInteractive(bulb, {
  title: 'Light source',
  tooltip: 'Unpolarized light source',
  body: 'An incandescent bulb emits light whose electric field oscillates in every possible orientation perpendicular to the direction of travel — it is unpolarized.'
});

/* ── unpolarized arrow star (enlarged so it reads like the source) ───────── */
const starGroup = new THREE.Group(); starGroup.position.set(-6.2, 0.35, 0); scene.add(starGroup);
[4, 25, 48, 70, 92, 115, 138, 161].forEach(function (a, i) {
  const ar = makeArrow(i % 2 ? 0.9 : 0.76, RED, 0.055);
  ar.rotation.x = THREE.MathUtils.degToRad(a);
  if (i % 3 === 1) ar.rotation.z = THREE.MathUtils.degToRad(i % 2 ? 22 : -22);
  starGroup.add(ar);
});
registerInteractive(starGroup, {
  title: 'Unpolarized light',
  tooltip: 'Electric field in all orientations',
  body: 'Each red double-headed arrow is one plane of oscillation. Pointing in all directions at once means the beam has no preferred polarization plane.'
});

/* ── beam ribbons (flat sheets in the XZ plane) ──────────────────────────── */
function ribbon(x0, x1, opacity) {
  const m = new THREE.Mesh(
    new THREE.PlaneGeometry(x1 - x0, 0.9),
    new THREE.MeshBasicMaterial({ color: BEAM, transparent: true, opacity: opacity, side: THREE.DoubleSide, depthWrite: false })
  );
  m.rotation.x = -Math.PI / 2;
  m.position.set((x0 + x1) / 2, 0, 0);
  scene.add(m);
  return m;
}
const ribbonIn = ribbon(-6.5, 2.6, 0.62);
const ribbonOut = ribbon(2.6, 6.0, 0.62);

/* ── polarizer ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── */
const polarizer = makeDisc(-4.6);
registerInteractive(polarizer, {
  title: 'Polarizer',
  tooltip: 'Transmits only one plane',
  body: 'The striped filter transmits only the component of the electric field parallel to its vertical transmission axis. Downstream, every arrow lies in one vertical plane: the light is plane-polarized.'
});

/* ── polarized (vertical) arrows ─────────────────────────────────────────── */
[-4.0, -3.3, -2.6].forEach(function (x) {
  const a = makeArrow(1.4, RED, 0.05);
  a.position.x = x; scene.add(a);
});

/* ── sample tube ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── */
const tube = new THREE.Group(); tube.position.x = -0.6; scene.add(tube);
const glassMat = new THREE.MeshPhysicalMaterial({
  color: 0xD6DED2, transparent: true, opacity: 0.38, roughness: 0.08,
  metalness: 0.0, clearcoat: 1.0, side: THREE.DoubleSide, depthWrite: false
});
const body = new THREE.Mesh(new THREE.CylinderGeometry(0.85, 0.85, 4.4, 48, 1, true), glassMat);
body.rotation.z = Math.PI / 2; tube.add(body);
[-1, 1].forEach(function (s) {
  const dome = new THREE.Mesh(new THREE.SphereGeometry(0.85, 40, 24, 0, Math.PI * 2, 0, Math.PI / 2), glassMat);
  dome.rotation.z = -s * Math.PI / 2;
  dome.position.x = s * 2.2; tube.add(dome);
});
const liquid = new THREE.Mesh(new THREE.CylinderGeometry(0.78, 0.78, 4.2, 40, 1, true),
  new THREE.MeshPhysicalMaterial({
    color: 0xBFD2BC, transparent: true, opacity: 0.26, roughness: 0.15,
    side: THREE.DoubleSide, depthWrite: false
  }));
liquid.rotation.z = Math.PI / 2; tube.add(liquid);
const stub = new THREE.Mesh(new THREE.CylinderGeometry(0.18, 0.18, 0.4, 20),
  new THREE.MeshStandardMaterial({ color: GREY, roughness: 0.35, metalness: 0.4 }));
stub.position.set(-0.6, 0.98, 0); tube.add(stub);
registerInteractive(body, {
  title: 'Sample tube containing organic molecules',
  tooltip: 'Optically active sample',
  body: 'A chiral (optically active) sample rotates the plane of polarization progressively as the light travels through it. The rotation grows with concentration and path length.'
});

/* ── arrows inside the tube (thicker, so their tilt reads clearly) ───────── */
const tubeArrows = [];
[-2.6, -1.9, -1.2, -0.5, 0.2, 0.9].forEach(function (x) {
  const a = makeArrow(1.35, RED, 0.055);
  a.position.x = x; scene.add(a); tubeArrows.push(a);
});

/* ── twisting polarization-plane ribbon through the tube ─────────────────── */
const TW_N = 40, TW_X0 = -2.65, TW_X1 = 0.95, TW_H = 0.62;
const twistGeom = new THREE.BufferGeometry();
const twistPos = new Float32Array((TW_N + 1) * 2 * 3);
twistGeom.setAttribute('position', new THREE.BufferAttribute(twistPos, 3));
(function () {
  const idx = [];
  for (let i = 0; i < TW_N; i++) {
    const a = i * 2, b = a + 1, c = a + 2, dd = a + 3;
    idx.push(a, b, c, b, dd, c);
  }
  twistGeom.setIndex(idx);
})();
const twistRibbon = new THREE.Mesh(twistGeom, new THREE.MeshBasicMaterial({
  color: RED, transparent: true, opacity: 0.17, side: THREE.DoubleSide, depthWrite: false
}));
scene.add(twistRibbon);

function updateTwist(sRad) {
  for (let i = 0; i <= TW_N; i++) {
    const f = i / TW_N;
    const x = TW_X0 + (TW_X1 - TW_X0) * f;
    const phi = -sRad * f;
    const cy = Math.cos(phi) * TW_H, cz = Math.sin(phi) * TW_H;
    const o = i * 6;
    twistPos[o] = x; twistPos[o + 1] = cy; twistPos[o + 2] = cz;
    twistPos[o + 3] = x; twistPos[o + 4] = -cy; twistPos[o + 5] = -cz;
  }
  twistGeom.attributes.position.needsUpdate = true;
  twistGeom.computeBoundingSphere();
}

/* ── analyzer ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── */
const analyzer = makeDisc(2.6);
registerInteractive(analyzer, {
  title: 'Analyzer',
  tooltip: 'Rotate to measure α',
  body: 'A second polarizing disc on a calibrated dial. The observer turns it until the transmitted light is brightest; the dial reading is the observed rotation α.'
});

/* ── exit region: heavy arrow, exit plane sheet, dashed reference, α arc ─── */
const exitArrow = makeArrow(2.1, RED, 0.1);
exitArrow.position.x = 4.4; scene.add(exitArrow);
registerInteractive(exitArrow, {
  title: 'Rotated plane of polarization',
  tooltip: 'Exit plane, tilted by α',
  body: 'The emerging light is still plane-polarized, but its plane has been turned through the angle α measured from the dashed vertical reference — that angle is what the polarimeter reports.'
});

const exitPlane = new THREE.Mesh(new THREE.PlaneGeometry(3.0, 1.9),
  new THREE.MeshBasicMaterial({ color: RED, transparent: true, opacity: 0.13, side: THREE.DoubleSide, depthWrite: false }));
exitPlane.position.x = 4.3;
const exitPlaneHolder = new THREE.Group(); exitPlaneHolder.add(exitPlane); scene.add(exitPlaneHolder);

greyLine([new THREE.Vector3(4.4, -1.45, 0), new THREE.Vector3(4.4, 1.6, 0)], 0x555555, true);

const arcGroup = new THREE.Group(); arcGroup.position.set(4.4, 0, 0); scene.add(arcGroup);
const arcMat = new THREE.MeshBasicMaterial({ color: 0x222222, depthTest: false });
const wedgeMat = new THREE.MeshBasicMaterial({
  color: 0x8C8C8C, transparent: true, opacity: 0.16, side: THREE.DoubleSide,
  depthWrite: false, depthTest: false
});
const arcHead = new THREE.Mesh(new THREE.ConeGeometry(0.085, 0.26, 16), arcMat);
arcHead.renderOrder = 7; arcGroup.add(arcHead);
let arcTube = null, wedgeMesh = null;
const _upV = new THREE.Vector3(0, 1, 0), _tanV = new THREE.Vector3();

function rebuildArc(rad) {
  if (arcTube) { arcGroup.remove(arcTube); arcTube.geometry.dispose(); arcTube = null; }
  if (wedgeMesh) { arcGroup.remove(wedgeMesh); wedgeMesh.geometry.dispose(); wedgeMesh = null; }
  const show = Math.abs(rad) > 0.05;
  arcGroup.visible = show;
  if (!show) return;
  const R = 1.3, n = 40, pts = [];
  for (let i = 0; i <= n; i++) {
    const t = rad * i / n;
    pts.push(new THREE.Vector3(0, R * Math.cos(t), -R * Math.sin(t)));
  }
  const curve = new THREE.CatmullRomCurve3(pts);
  arcTube = new THREE.Mesh(new THREE.TubeGeometry(curve, 56, 0.028, 8, false), arcMat);
  arcTube.renderOrder = 7; arcGroup.add(arcTube);

  // faint wedge fill so α stays readable even when seen obliquely
  const Rw = 1.14, verts = [], idx = [];
  verts.push(0, 0, 0);
  for (let i = 0; i <= n; i++) {
    const t = rad * i / n;
    verts.push(0, Rw * Math.cos(t), -Rw * Math.sin(t));
  }
  for (let i = 1; i <= n; i++) idx.push(0, i, i + 1);
  const wg = new THREE.BufferGeometry();
  wg.setAttribute('position', new THREE.Float32BufferAttribute(verts, 3));
  wg.setIndex(idx);
  wedgeMesh = new THREE.Mesh(wg, wedgeMat);
  wedgeMesh.renderOrder = 6; arcGroup.add(wedgeMesh);

  const sgn = Math.sign(rad);
  arcHead.position.set(0, R * Math.cos(rad), -R * Math.sin(rad));
  _tanV.set(0, -Math.sin(rad) * sgn, -Math.cos(rad) * sgn).normalize();
  arcHead.quaternion.setFromUnitVectors(_upV, _tanV);
}

/* ── observer eye (billboarded pictogram) ────────────────────────────────── */
